 "name": "Crowding at Tulum: Acceptable Number of Visitors (Photo Rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Enseñat-Soberanis, F., & Blanco-Gregory, R. (2022). Crowding Perception at the Archaeological Site of Tulum, Mexico: A Key Indicator for Sustainable Cultural Tourism. Land 11:1651, doi:10.3390/land11101651. Visitors rate how acceptable the number of people is in six photographs of the trail intersection in front of the Temple of the Frescoes (edited in Photoshop by the authors to show 0, 21, 42, 63, 84 and 105 visitors) on the −4 to +4 normative scale (Heberlein & Vaske). Part 1 asks about crowding problems and whether the site is over-utilized; Part 3 records gender, age, education, residence (Mexico / abroad) and first visit. Analysis as in the paper: mean acceptability per photo against number of visitors (social norm curve), zero crossing = maximum acceptable number (57 people in the original), national vs international visitors by t-test. Photographs: paper Figure 3 (CC BY 4.0), used with credit; the template is removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the original was a face-to-face Spanish/English paper questionnaire given at the site exit in 2018; this is an online English version; (2) the wording of the Part 1 and profile questions is not printed in the paper and was reconstructed from the reported results (problem categories, over-utilization, age bands, degree); (3) the paper describes the scale both as acceptability (+4 to −4) and as 1 = not at all crowded to 9 = extremely crowded; this template uses the acceptability labels from very unacceptable (−4) to very acceptable (+4); (4) photographs are taken from the published figure, so they have lower resolution than the originals, and all six are shown on one page in order 1 to 6.",
